       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Your confirmation didn't change the safety problem. 50 mg is five times the maximum recommended daily dose, and I haven't heard a clinical reason for it. I also can't tell whether a supervising physician has reviewed it. A dose like this could cause profound hypotension and shock, and the effects can last a long time because of amlodipine's long half-life.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** Tell me 5 mg or 10 mg once daily and I'll enter it right away.\n- **Have your attending or resident review it.** If they want 50 mg for a specific reason, the supervising prescriber should enter or co-sign it after talking with pharmacy.\n- **Check the intent.** If the goal is stronger blood pressure control, the usual approach is to add a second agent, such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide, rather than go above 10 mg of amlodipine.\n\nIf you'd like, I can enter a standard dose now."
      }
